          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a clinician writes diabetes in one place and cataract in another, does the \"with\" convention connect them?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: the retrieved sources don't confirm this for diabetes and cataract.** They describe the \"with\" convention in general, using diabetes and kidney disease as the example, but they don't mention cataract.\n\nAccording to the coding summary \"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories,\" the \"with\" convention works like this. Where the alphabetic index links two conditions with the word \"with,\" the coding system reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the link. The record doesn't have to state it. The same passage says the presumption holds unless the documentation states the two conditions are unrelated. Its example is type 2 diabetes listed with chronic kidney disease, which is coded as diabetic kidney disease.\n\nThe sources don't say whether the index has a \"with\" entry that links diabetes and cataract, so I can't tell you whether that presumption would apply. Coding decisions like this depend on the specific index entries and on what the clinician wrote. A medical coder or the clinician who wrote the record can confirm how it would be coded.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
